course: dictionary
lesson: 4272
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so read the words around them to find the right one. «Add soap and the grease slides away.» Soap and washing tell you that grease is oily dirt on things, not fat in a cake tin.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence to check it. «Each soap particle has a water-loving head and an oil-loving tail; the tails grab the grease and the heads pull it into the water.» Grease is the oily stuff that soap lifts away, so the meaning fits.
explain: Look at the job the word does. As a verb, to grease a tin means to put fat or butter on it so the cake does not stick. The phrase elbow grease is different: it means hard work, not fat.
fact: Grease can be a thick, oily substance, such as oil that helps machine parts move smoothly.
fact: Grease can also mean soft animal fat, especially after cooking, like bacon grease.
fact: As a verb, to grease something means to put fat, butter or oil on it, like greasing a cake tin.
fact: Grease reduces friction, so parts slide smoothly; but grease on clothes needs soap to clean it.
fact: «Grease someone's palm» is slang meaning to bribe them.
exercise: «Add soap and the grease slides away.» What is grease in this sentence? | A cold drink | *Oily dirt on things | Hard work | A baking tin
exercise: Ms Lee says, «Grease the tin before you pour in the mix.» What does grease mean here? | To paint it bright colours | *To put fat or butter on it so the cake does not stick | To wash it with cold water
exercise: Dad says he needs elbow grease to clean the car. What does elbow grease mean? | *Hard work | Fat from the elbow | A soft oil for bikes
test: In science, what does grease reduce so that parts slide smoothly? | Light | *Friction | Heat | Sound
test: Which phrase is slang meaning to bribe someone? | Elbow grease | Grease the wheels | *Grease someone's palm | Grease the tin
exam: Which use of grease is a verb? | *Grease the baking tin with butter before the cake goes in | Bacon grease left in the pan | A thick grease on the engine that keeps it running | Grease stains on my school shirt
